# kasan.config — potentially CONFORMING (pending full-config verification)
#
# Profile:        kasan (memory-safety discovery and validation)
# Kbase:          r54p0-01eac0 (primary)  [+ patches/virtual-device/ 0001-0006]
# Kernel:         6.12.111 — the pinned, VERIFIED-working kernel
#                 (kernel/sources/kernel.pin; baseline built on it, F-18).
#                 NOTE: the newest LTS 6.18.54 does NOT build (F-16), so "latest
#                 LTS" is not available for this project.
# Patch set:      vendor/virtual-device/ 0001-0006
# Scope class:    potentially IN SCOPE — every CONFIG_KASAN* option is EXPLICITLY
#                 permitted by the Arm allowlist (program-scope.md §5), except
#                 CONFIG_KASAN_*_TEST which must be n. Conforming status is
#                 PROVISIONAL: it holds only if the COMPLETE final config stays
#                 within the allowlist. Do not call it conforming until the
#                 savedefconfig diff (project spec §18) verifies every delta.
# Generated from: Arm's virtual-platform guide Kbase options + CONFIG_KASAN*
#                 symbols named from general mainline knowledge.
# Validation:     BUILT + BOOTED 2026-10-01 on 6.12.111. 16/16 fragment symbols
#                 took effect; `kasan: KernelAddressSanitizer initialized` at
#                 0.70 s; EL0 probe passed=0x1ff failed=0x000; ZERO KASAN
#                 reports; mali_kbase.ko grew 2.4 MB -> 5.1 MB (inlining + the
#                 redzone-instrumented module). Logs: research/boot-logs/*-kasan-BOOT.log.
# Config hash:    merged .config 5af7eca01b287e883fd0ca51fe301b6782105f38b085821e2e2ae03db707aa99
#                 (build/logs/kasan.log step 5). Build tree was PRUNED afterwards to
#                 free disk, so build/kasan/.config is absent by design; the hash
#                 above is the record. The boot log is the evidence.
# Bundle:        NOT packaged. No artifacts/kasan/ exists yet.
#
# NOT a valid .config. NOT the output of make defconfig/savedefconfig.

# Purpose: memory-safety discovery, and — unlike kcov — this profile can double
# as a validation environment because ASAN is on the allowlist.

# --- Linux AddressSanitizer (ALLOWLISTED per program-scope.md §5) ---------
# All are CONFIG_KASAN* and therefore permitted. Avoid any CONFIG_KASAN_*_TEST
# (must be n).
CONFIG_KASAN=y
CONFIG_KASAN_GENERIC=y
CONFIG_KASAN_INLINE=y

# --- Kbase driver options -------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_MALI_MIDGARD=m
CONFIG_MALI_CSF_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_MALI_EXPERT=y
CONFIG_MALI_NO_MALI=y
# CONFIG_MALI_REAL_HW is not set
CONFIG_MALI_NO_MALI_DEFAULT_GPU="tDRx"
CONFIG_MALI_PLATFORM_NAME="vexpress"

# MALI_NO_MALI choice: RESOLVED, no longer deferred (F-21).
# This fragment previously asserted `# CONFIG_MALI_REAL_HW is not set` while
# deferring the choice, i.e. it named NO choice member at all. Kconfig cannot
# represent "no member selected": `drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/Kconfig` gives the
# `Mali HW backend` choice `default MALI_REAL_HW`, so the deferred state
# silently resolved to MALI_REAL_HW=y — the opposite of the stated intent.
# The tool-level cause was the fragment merge in build.sh step 4 (F-20).
#
# The experiment this comment deferred on is now answered by the step-7 boot
# evidence (F-19, artifacts/baseline): MALI_NO_MALI is the only backend
# that yields a LOADABLE module and a REACHABLE /dev/mali0 + ioctl surface on
# this host. VERIFIED for MALI_NO_MALI (module loads, r54p0-01eac0, drm minor
# 10/258 created, EL0 probe passed 0x1ff). Whether MALI_REAL_HW would also
# probe under the fake `vexpress` platform device is UNKNOWN / NOT_TESTED, and
# is deliberately not claimed either way.
#
# So: same choice as baseline/kcov/debug, and the same tDRx target fixed by
# DECISION-2. Choosing NO_MALI=y here is a HARNESS requirement, not a
# conformant-validation claim: per DECISION-1 this whole profile is
# DISCOVERY-ONLY. KASAN itself stays allowlisted (§5).

# --- Kbase debug: MANDATORILY n ------------------------------------------
# Program policy requires CONFIG_MALI_DEBUG=n and does NOT list MALI_DEBUG among
# the changeable options (research/program-scope.md §8.3). An earlier INFERRED
# note claimed MALI_DEBUG=y was needed for ASAN interaction; that is WITHDRAWN.
CONFIG_MALI_DEBUG=n

# --- Kernel hard build gates, set explicitly ------------------------------
# VERIFIED: midgard/Kbuild raises $(error ...) for FIVE unconditional symbols at
# lines 29,33,37,41,45. The test is `ifeq ($(CONFIG_X),n)`, which does NOT catch
# an unset symbol. DEVFREQ_GOV_SIMPLE_ONDEMAND was MISSING from an earlier
# revision of these fragments; see findings.md F-3 and F-11.
# F-26 INERT: this line cannot take effect. DMA_SHARED_BUFFER is a prompt-less
# `bool` (drivers/base/Kconfig:200) and is therefore unreachable from a config
# fragment. It IS y in the built .config because Kbase's own Kconfig selects it
# (midgard/Kconfig:23) -- not because of this line. The gate is genuinely met;
# the line is kept only as the record of the requirement. Do not read its
# presence as evidence that this profile controls the symbol.
CONFIG_DMA_SHARED_BUFFER=y
CONFIG_PM_DEVFREQ=y
CONFIG_DEVFREQ_THERMAL=y
CONFIG_DEVFREQ_GOV_SIMPLE_ONDEMAND=y
CONFIG_FW_LOADER=y

# --- UNRESOLVED, for the build phase --------------------------------------
# - Whether to use MALI_NO_MALI or the real path for this profile.
# - KASAN heap/quarantine tuning for the driver's DMA mappings.
# - Exact kernel version; complete configuration.
# - Whether any non-KASAN delta is introduced and must be classified.
